==English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|bestial}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|bēstiālis}}, from {{m|la|bēstia||beast}} (whence English {{m|en|beast}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈbɛst͡ʃəl/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|/ˈbiːstiəl/|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|literally|_|and|_|figuratively}} [[beast|Beast]]-like
#* c. '''1604''', {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|Othello}}'', Act II, Scene 3, [http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=othello&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]
#*: Reputation, reputation, reputation! O, I have lost my reputation! I have lost the immortal part of myself, and what remains is '''bestial'''.
#* '''1674''', {{w|John Milton}}, ''{{w|Paradise Lost}}'', Book 4, lines 753-4, [https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Paradise_Lost_(1674)/Book_IV]
#*: By thee adulterous lust was driven from men /
#*: Among the '''bestial''' herds to range {{...}}
#* '''1886''', {{w|Robert Louis Stevenson}}, ''{{w|Strange Case of Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde}}'', [http://www.gutenberg.org/files/43/43-h/43-h.htm]
#*: This familiar that I called out of my own soul, and sent forth alone to do his good pleasure, was a being inherently malign and villainous; his every act and thought centered on self; drinking pleasure with '''bestial''' avidity from any degree of torture to another; relentless like a man of stone.

